WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.810
<v Krista>In this video, we're talking about signs</v>

2
00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:04.230
of fractions and how to use signs of fractions

3
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.430
to simplify expressions like this one,

4
00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:08.190
where we have the difference of two fractions.

5
00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:11.100
We have the fraction one over x minus three

6
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.470
minus this other fraction, four over negative x plus three.

7
00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:16.530
So the thing we have to remember

8
00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.570
when we're talking about signs of fractions is

9
00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.380
that every fraction has three signs associated with it.

10
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.440
It has the sign in the numerator,

11
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.040
so the numerator can have a positive

12
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.440
or negative sign in front of it.

13
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.900
It has a sign associated with the denominator,

14
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.070
so a positive or negative sign in front of the denominator.

15
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.300
And it can have a sign out in front of the fraction,

16
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.000
and that can be positive or negative.

17
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.730
If there's no sign written,

18
00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:42.850
it's implied that there's a positive sign.

19
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.750
So, for example, on both of our fractions here,

20
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.820
the sign of the numerator is positive, right?

21
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.980
Nobody wrote plus one or plus four.

22
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.670
It's just implied that it's positive.

23
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.860
Because it's not written, it's an implied positive sign.

24
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.700
Otherwise, if it's a negative sign, it will be written.

25
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.660
So coming back to our original problem,

26
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.560
what do we know about adding or subtracting fractions?

27
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.630
Well, we have to have a common denominator

28
00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.570
in order to add or subtract fractions.

29
00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.760
The denominator of our first fraction is x minus three.

30
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.780
The denominator of our second fraction

31
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.380
is negative x plus three.

32
00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.170
So, in other words, over here we've got a positive x,

33
00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.030
over here we have a negative x.

34
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.120
In our first fraction, we have a negative three.

35
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.700
In our second fraction, we have a positive three.

36
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.000
So the signs are exactly flipped.

37
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.090
If we want to find a common denominator,

38
00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.240
we're going to have to get the signs to match,

39
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.960
and the way that we do that is with signs of fractions.

40
00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.970
Because what we need to remember is

41
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.480
you can always change any two signs of the fraction

42
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.670
without changing the fraction's identity.

43
00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.990
So, for example, if we have a positive,

44
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.000
positive one over negative two, right,

45
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.380
this would actually just be written as one over negative two

46
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.720
because we don't write our positive signs.

47
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.670
They're implied.

48
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.730
We can change two of the signs

49
00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.870
without changing the fraction's identity.

50
00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.340
So, for example, if we wanted to change this,

51
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.420
we wanted to make this sign out in front negative,

52
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.260
that would be changing one of the signs.

53
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.520
Let's say we keep the positive sign in the numerator,

54
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.840
and we change the sign in the denominator

55
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.180
to a positive two from a negative two.

56
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.210
So we changed this sign, and we changed this sign.

57
00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.370
We changed exactly two of the signs,

58
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.930
and let's see what we get here.

59
00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.860
We have a positive and a positive that's implied.

60
00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.760
This is just negative 1/2.

61
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.340
That's the same thing as one over negative two.

62
00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.780
So we can change exactly two signs

63
00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.700
without changing the value of the fraction.

64
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.050
So here we want to change two signs to try

65
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.750
to get the signs of the denominator to be the same.

66
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:37.750
But if we change two signs,

67
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.530
we won't change the value of the fraction.

68
00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.830
So what we want to do is make the denominator,

69
00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.400
we want to put parentheses around the denominator like this,

70
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.020
and now this is an implied positive sign

71
00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.260
in front of the parentheses here.

72
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.810
We want to change this to a negative sign

73
00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.430
because applying this negative sign to the denominator

74
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.420
will change this negative x to a positive

75
00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.080
and the positive three to a negative,

76
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.360
and then we'll get our denominators to match.

77
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.850
So we changed the sign of the denominator

78
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.520
from a positive to a negative,

79
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.120
but now we have to change one of the other signs.

80
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.170
This is the sign that's in front of the fraction,

81
00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.440
the negative sign, so let's go ahead and change that

82
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.250
from a negative to a positive.

83
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.390
We could have instead changed this sign

84
00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:16.960
from a positive to a negative.

85
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.940
The point is you have to change exactly two of the signs.

86
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.840
So we changed the denominator and the one out in front,

87
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.350
and we left the numerator alone

88
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.120
because we need to change two.

89
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.570
So now when we rewrite this, we have one over x minus three.

90
00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.490
And then here we're going to have plus,

91
00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.810
and we don't need to imply this positive four here.

92
00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.260
We just say four over.

93
00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.920
Now we use the distributive property.

94
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.160
We apply this negative sign to both of our terms.

95
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.080
So negative, negative x,

96
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.820
those negative signs are going to cancel,

97
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.710
and we're going to get a positive x.

98
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.550
And then we're going to have negative one essentially

99
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.660
times a positive three gives us a negative three.

100
00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.260
So we get x minus three.

101
00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.890
And now we have a common denominator.

102
00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.140
So we can use our common denominator x minus three.

103
00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.320
And then in our numerator,

104
00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.980
we just combine the two numerators.

105
00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.260
So we have one plus four,

106
00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:07.480
one plus four into the numerator,

107
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.340
and then the solution becomes five

108
00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.560
over quantity x minus three.

109
00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.240
So this is just one example,

110
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.020
but again the important thing to remember is

111
00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.160
that every fraction has three signs associated with it,

112
00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.140
one out in front, one in the numerator,

113
00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.630
and one in the denominator.

114
00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.640
And you can change any two signs of the fraction

115
00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.950
without changing the value of the fraction,

116
00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:29.760
and sometimes that can be useful

117
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.713
when we're trying to simplify expressions like this one.

